1 READ

   Reads a record from a file into a symbol.

   Format

     READ logical-name[:] symbol-name

2 Parameters

 logical-name[:]

   A file OPEN opened, or SYS$INPUT, SYS$COMMAND, SYS$OUTPUT, or
   SYS$ERROR, which need no OPEN.

 symbol-name

   The symbol, local to the command level, that gets the record as it
   is: its case and blanks are kept, and no symbol substitution is
   done.

2 Description

   In a command procedure, READ SYS$INPUT reads the procedure's next
   line, whatever it is.  At the terminal, and from SYS$COMMAND
   anywhere, READ prompts at the terminal and reads a line; Ctrl/Z is
   the end of the file.

   At the end of the file, the procedure goes to the /END_OF_FILE label,
   or failing that the /ERROR label, with $STATUS RMS$_EOF; with
   neither, RMS-E-EOF is an error, and the ON action is taken.  A READ
   that fails leaves its symbol as it was.  A record longer than 2048
   bytes is cut to 2048, with RMS-W-RTB.

   On an indexed file, READ reads the next record in the order of the
   key it last used, the primary key at first.  After a keyed READ, READ
   goes on in that key's order.

2 Qualifiers
/DELETE

   Deletes the record read, on an indexed file opened /READ/WRITE, and
   leaves $STATUS RMS$_NORMAL.  On another file the symbol gets the
   record and READ fails with RMS-F-FAC.

/END_OF_FILE=label

   In a command procedure, goes to the label at the end of the file.

/ERROR=label

   In a command procedure, goes to the label if the READ fails, or at
   the end of the file when there is no /END_OF_FILE.

/INDEX=n

   Chooses the key of an indexed file that /KEY looks up and READ
   follows, 0 being the primary key.  It stays for later READs of the
   file until another /INDEX.

/KEY=string

   Reads the record of an indexed file whose key is the string.  A
   string shorter than the key matches the key's first characters.

/[NO]LOCK

   On a file opened with /SHARE=WRITE, READ locks the record until the
   next operation on the file, and another READ of it is RMS-E-RLK.
   /NOLOCK reads it without a lock, even one another READ holds.

/MATCH=keyword

   How /KEY's string matches: EQ, an equal key (the default); GE, the
   first key equal or next in the key's own order; GT, the first next.

/PROMPT=string

   The prompt for a READ from the terminal.  The default is "Data:" and
   a tab.

/TIME_OUT=n

   Accepted, and changes nothing.

2 Examples

   1.$ OPEN IN NAMES.DAT
     $ LOOP:
     $ READ/END_OF_FILE=DONE IN NAME
     $ WRITE SYS$OUTPUT "Hello, ", NAME
     $ GOTO LOOP
     $ DONE:
     $ CLOSE IN

     Greets each name in NAMES.DAT.

   2.$ READ/KEY="SMITH"/MATCH=GE PHONES ENTRY

     Reads the first record of PHONES whose primary key is SMITH or
     follows it.
